meta {
  name: Run AI Review Batch
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: http://localhost:8088/api/admin-product/resolutions/ai-review/run
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "maxPerRun": 5
  }
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Runs the same batch the 3 AM scheduler runs, on demand. Intake is
  `priority DESC` — the order the admin queue shows — so this and a human work
  one list from the same end.

  Body fields are all optional; anything omitted falls back to
  `resolution.automation.ai`.

  - `maxPerRun` — rows this run. Start small.
  - `minPriority` — priority floor for intake.
  - `executeDestructive` — can only *tighten* the configured value. Passing
    `true` when the config says `false` does NOT enable merges; a request must
    not be able to turn on the switch that deletes products.

  Every reviewed row is written to either way: the verdict lands on `aiReview`
  and is appended to the `decisions` log. `executeActions` and
  `executeDestructive` gate whether it is *acted on*, never whether it is stored.

  The summary distinguishes:
  - `executed` — a recommendation was carried out.
  - `advisory` — judged and left for a human (low/medium confidence, a kill
    switch, or an action the row could not legally take).
  - `abstained` — the model declined to call it. Counts as advisory too.
  - `skippedStale` — the row changed between intake and the verdict, so the
    action was discarded. A steady non-zero number here means rows are being
    re-scraped mid-run.
  - `capped` — stopped on the row limit or the cost cap, so more work is waiting.

  Which rows are skipped entirely, and why, is in `findAiReviewBatch`: `failed`
  rows (environmental, not judgement), human-touched rows, unclassified rows, and
  `insufficient_evidence` rows — the last because there is genuinely nothing to
  judge, so a call would buy an abstain at full price.
}
